# IMPERIA CW — Cold War: israel 1970 — programme narrative.
"""Party-specific programme prose. Rewards remain owned by revised_rewards.py."""

ROWS = """p0_ea_deepen|Teachers for Religious Vocational Schools|Religious vocational schools need instructors who can teach industrial skills without abandoning their communities' educational commitments. Preparing those teachers will connect workshop qualifications to the institutions through which Mafdal already organises public life.
p0_eb_deepen|Services for the Southern Communities|Religious welfare organisations cannot serve the development towns without dependable roads and local facilities. Connecting these communities to public services will give the municipal network a practical foundation beyond charitable appeals.
p0_ca_deepen|A Trained National-Service Cadre|Bnei Akiva and the service institutions must prepare young people for responsibilities that extend beyond mobilisation. Common training standards will make the religious-Zionist service framework a more dependable part of national defence.
p0_cb_deepen|Supply the Border Communities|Border communities need reliable connections to the forces responsible for their defence. Coordinating supplies and local preparations will turn the settlement movement's security claims into an organisation that can sustain its responsibilities.
p0_finish|Religion and the Republic|Mafdal must show that religious education, community organisation and national service can support the same state. The cabinet's chosen settlement will determine how those obligations are shared without dissolving the movement's distinctive institutions into the larger parties.
p1_ea_deepen|Commerce on the Tel Aviv Model|The Liberal wing of Gahal sees commercial employment as a route to prosperity beyond protected enterprises. A new office district in Tel Aviv will give that argument a concrete base of firms, services and technical work.
p1_eb_deepen|Strategic Industry in Haifa|Gahal's economic debate need not end with the disposal of every public enterprise. Investment in Haifa will demonstrate whether strategic state ownership can coexist with a cabinet otherwise committed to greater scope for private initiative.
p1_ca_deepen|Parliamentary Scrutiny of the Executive|Herut's experience in opposition gives parliamentary scrutiny a particular importance within Gahal. Stronger safeguards and a more capable civil administration will require ministers to accept procedures that constrain their own government as well as its opponents.
p1_cb_deepen|A Cohesive Security Establishment|A firm security policy requires units that can remain organised and supplied in the field. Gahal will direct attention towards the daily work of sustaining the force, giving its political commitments an operational foundation.
p1_finish|The Conservative Transfer of Power|Herut and the Liberals must turn an opposition alliance into a government that can endure. Their chosen economic programme and civic settlement will shape a common record of administration, while their separate traditions continue to test the cabinet's cohesion.
p2_ea_deepen|Room for Independent Businesses|Independent shopkeepers and service firms need commercial space that is not reserved for the largest political or economic organisations. A Tel Aviv office district will support the Liberals' promise to broaden access to productive private enterprise.
p2_eb_deepen|Export Production in Haifa|Liberal support for enterprise must also reach firms that manufacture rather than merely trade. A modern industrial investment in Haifa will connect capital formation to production methods capable of sustaining competitive export employment.
p2_ca_deepen|An Accountable Liberal Civil Service|Civil rights need officials who apply public rules consistently. Auditing the administration will bring the Independent Liberals' constitutional commitments into everyday dealings with the state and reduce the burden of unnecessary bureaucracy.
p2_cb_deepen|Defensive Analysis and Secure Communications|A liberal government cannot neglect national defence, but it can insist on careful assessment rather than political reflex. Technical preparation and secure communications will support a security policy grounded in professional judgement.
p2_finish|A Liberal Republic|The Independent Liberals seek a place for rights, research and productive enterprise outside the larger political blocs. Their programme must demonstrate that an independent parliamentary voice can administer the state and sustain confidence without surrendering its principles to coalition convenience.
p3_ea_deepen|Modern Workshops for the Histadrut Economy|The institutions of Labor Zionism must adapt their industrial base to a changing economy. Investment in Haifa will give workers and managers a practical test of modernisation within the enterprises around which the Alignment's social influence has grown.
p3_eb_deepen|Irrigation for Cooperative Development|Cooperative settlement depends on productive land and dependable water rather than political symbolism alone. An irrigated district in the Negev will connect the Alignment's rural institutions to a renewed programme of agricultural development.
p3_ca_deepen|Public Services in the Development Towns|The social compact must reach households beyond the established metropolitan centres. Better local connections and public-service provision will give the development towns a more tangible share in the prosperity promised by the Alignment.
p3_cb_deepen|Room for Negotiating Strength|A cabinet that relies on negotiating strength must retain the freedom to choose its own position. Political consolidation and greater diplomatic independence will help the Alignment conduct talks without treating every outside preference as an instruction.
p3_finish|A Second Building of Israel|Labor and Mapam must reconcile industrial modernisation, cooperative traditions and the needs of communities that feel excluded from both. The government will be judged on whether its chosen programme renews the institutions of Labor Zionism rather than merely preserving their authority.
p4_ea_deepen|Workers Trained for Public Enterprise|Parliamentary communism must show that public ownership can improve work as well as change its formal direction. Technical education and stronger enterprise productivity will give the Jewish-Arab workers' alliance a practical economic record.
p4_eb_deepen|A Public Industrial Base in Haifa|Central planning requires factories capable of carrying out its priorities. A public industrial investment in Haifa will place responsibility for employment and production directly on the government and the workers' organisations that support it.
p4_ca_deepen|Equal Access to Municipal Services|Equal citizenship will remain an abstraction if neighbourhoods receive unequal public services. The communist cabinet will strengthen municipal provision so that cooperation between Jewish and Arab workers has an everyday institutional basis.
p4_cb_deepen|Technical Preparation for Soviet Armour|Closer military cooperation with Moscow requires crews and technical organisations able to understand contemporary armoured designs. Research preparation will develop that competence without assuming that a diplomatic orientation alone delivers finished equipment.
p4_finish|A Socialist Peace Settlement|Rakah's government must hold together its class programme, Jewish-Arab equality and the security choices made during its term. A socialist settlement will depend on capable public institutions and services that make cooperation more durable than a parliamentary declaration.
p5_ea_deepen|Technical Knowledge between Councils|Autonomous producers' councils need ways to share expertise without surrendering every decision to a central ministry. Joint technical training and industrial research will help local workplaces learn from one another's experience.
p5_eb_deepen|Cooperative Cultivation in the Negev|A cooperative federation needs a rural economy alongside its municipal councils. Investment in a Negev agricultural district will give producers a common project whose operation depends on sustained coordination rather than a hierarchy of ministerial orders.
p5_ca_deepen|Local Representation within the Federation|A federal settlement will endure only if communities trust the bodies that represent them. Clear local responsibilities and dependable institutions can protect the agreement against the suspicion that one centre will eventually take back the authority it has promised.
p5_cb_deepen|Shared Services for a Common Republic|A common civic republic must provide services that residents can use regardless of community. Building that administrative capacity will give the council left's constitutional ambitions a practical test in daily public provision.
p5_finish|A Federation of Municipalities|The council left seeks a federation held together by cooperation among producers and municipalities. Its chosen economic and civic institutions must provide enough coordination to sustain development while leaving local bodies with a meaningful role in their own affairs.
p6_ea_deepen|Commercial Space in the Negev|Family firms in the development towns need premises and business services as well as political recognition. A municipal office district will give the traditionalist alliance a concrete investment in the southern communities it claims to represent.
p6_eb_deepen|Connect the Southern Development Towns|Municipal development funds must reach communities whose distance from established centres raises the cost of work and public services. Better infrastructure will make those towns a more practical base for households and local employers.
p6_ca_deepen|Teachers and Trades for the Periphery|Young people in traditional communities should not have to choose between retaining their social ties and acquiring useful qualifications. Teacher preparation and vocational education will broaden their opportunities within the development towns.
p6_cb_deepen|Community Welfare Administration|Traditional social partnership needs organisations that can administer help reliably. Strengthening community welfare will allow local institutions to respond to households without making every need depend on a personal connection to a political intermediary.
p6_finish|The Rise of the Development Towns|The traditionalist alliance must turn the periphery's political voice into dependable work and services. Its programme will be judged by whether development towns gain institutions of their own, rather than another channel through which established centres distribute favours.
p7_ea_deepen|Industry under Professional Direction|The directorate's claim to competence will be tested by the enterprises it develops. A public industrial investment in Haifa will give its planners a clear responsibility for production and employment beyond the language of emergency administration.
p7_eb_deepen|The State Corporations' Building Programme|The directorate will channel industrial investment through the corporations and interests that sustain its authority. New capacity can strengthen production, but the distribution of its benefits will also expose the relationship between economic development and political patronage.
p7_ca_deepen|Audit the Civilian Transition|A promised return to civilian government needs credible scrutiny of the administration that manages it. An effective audit will test the directorate's willingness to submit its record to standards that are independent of its own claims.
p7_cb_deepen|The Presidential Planning Office|An enduring presidency will bring administration and construction planning closer to the executive. Ministers and technical staff will work through a more concentrated centre, tying the development programme to the president's continued authority.
p7_finish|The Directorate State|The directorate must now answer for the institutions built under its exceptional authority. Its economic priorities and constitutional choice will determine whether technical administration prepares a civilian settlement or becomes the foundation of a lasting presidential order.
p12_ea_deepen|Commerce for the Northern Municipalities|Arab municipalities need a stronger commercial base alongside their public institutions. New office capacity in the north will support local firms and give municipal representatives a practical answer to the demand for productive employment.
p12_eb_deepen|Roads between Communities and Services|Municipal welfare organisations cannot compensate indefinitely for weak physical connections. Improvements to roads and service links will help local institutions reach residents and make their public responsibilities easier to sustain.
p12_ca_deepen|Public Scrutiny of Civic Institutions|Participation in common civic institutions must include the power to question their administration. Public scrutiny will give municipal representatives a way to insist that equal citizenship is reflected in the state's everyday conduct.
p12_cb_deepen|Capable Autonomous Municipal Services|Local autonomy requires more than a legal boundary around municipal powers. Better organised services will give councils the capacity to exercise those powers and build residents' confidence in their community institutions.
p12_finish|A Political Voice for the Municipalities|The municipal reform list must translate parliamentary influence into institutions that residents can rely on. Its chosen settlement between common citizenship and local authority will define how Arab municipalities participate in the wider political order.
p13_ea_deepen|Trades alongside Religious Learning|Orthodox families need ways to acquire technical qualifications without abandoning religious study. A sustained training framework will allow community institutions to support productive work while retaining the educational commitments at the heart of the parliamentary bloc.
p13_eb_deepen|Professional Community Care|Charitable provision must manage increasingly complex health and welfare needs. Better administration will help community organisations use their resources consistently and reduce dependence on improvised arrangements when households require continuing assistance.
p13_ca_deepen|A Parliamentary Religious Settlement|The religious status quo needs a workable agreement among parties that disagree about the state's character. A durable parliamentary settlement will give Orthodox representatives a basis for defending their institutions while sustaining the government they have helped to form.
p13_cb_deepen|Civilian Service Links for Communities|Voluntary service will be useful only if it connects participants to real local needs. Better links between development-town communities and their service organisations will give the Orthodox compromise a practical public purpose.
p13_finish|A Sustainable Religious Compromise|Agudat Israel and Poalei Agudat must defend their communities while meeting the responsibilities of parliamentary government. Their chosen balance of education, welfare and public service will determine whether religious accommodation can sustain confidence beyond the immediate coalition bargain.
p14_ea_deepen|Professional Management in Haifa|Public enterprises need managers able to answer for production rather than political sponsorship. Industrial investment in Haifa will give the pragmatic right an opportunity to demonstrate that the state's economic role can be administered with clear commercial responsibilities.
p14_eb_deepen|Commercial Initiative in Tel Aviv|Private commerce offers the pragmatic cabinet another route to employment and development. A Tel Aviv office district will create room for that initiative while testing the government's ability to support enterprise without extending unnecessary administrative control.
p14_ca_deepen|Sustain a Defensive Posture|A restrained security policy still requires reliable supplies and a force organised for its assigned tasks. Reducing the practical burden of sustaining that posture will help the cabinet keep readiness within its economic means.
p14_cb_deepen|Public Confidence for Negotiations|Territorial negotiations require a domestic political foundation as well as diplomatic contacts. The pragmatic cabinet will build confidence in its approach so that talks can proceed without every concession becoming a crisis of authority.
p14_finish|Pragmatism as Statecraft|The State List and its partners seek a government judged by workable administration rather than a comprehensive ideological programme. Their record in enterprise management and security policy will determine whether pragmatism can command a stable parliamentary constituency.
p15_ea_deepen|Supervised Investment in Tel Aviv|Diaspora investment must create productive facilities under terms the state can supervise. A new Tel Aviv office district will test whether the development alliance can attract capital while keeping public responsibilities separate from the interests of its sponsors.
p15_eb_deepen|The Haifa Industrial Concession|Privileged concessions will be justified by their supporters as a faster route to industrial development. Investment in Haifa will give the sponsors new productive capacity while making the political distribution of opportunity increasingly visible.
p15_ca_deepen|A Professional Contract Administration|Transparent investment contracts depend on officials who can inspect and enforce them. A more capable administration will reduce routine burdens while giving the development alliance a credible alternative to informal bargains with favoured investors.
p15_cb_deepen|Construction through the Patronage Network|The alliance will consolidate the network of notables and investors through which its building programme operates. This can strengthen its political organisation and delivery capacity, while tying development more closely to continued access to the governing coalition.
p15_finish|The Investment State|The development alliance must decide what its investment record says about the state it governs. Supervised capital and privileged concessions lead to different relationships between public authority and private wealth, even when both promise growth and new employment.
p16_ea_deepen|Qualifications for Civilian Electronics|A civil-rights economy needs productive opportunities alongside constitutional promises. Technical qualifications and research in civilian electronics will give the Free Citizens movement a development programme that reaches beyond the defence establishment and established political enterprises.
p16_eb_deepen|Commercial Capacity without the Old Bureaucracy|Removing administrative barriers must be followed by opportunities for firms to operate. New commercial offices in Tel Aviv will give the movement's economic alternative a practical base rather than leaving reform confined to the repeal of rules.
p16_ca_deepen|Independent Scrutiny of the Secular State|Secular government must be accountable in practice, including when its ministers claim to act in the name of civil freedom. An independent public audit will test the administration against standards that do not depend on loyalty to the movement.
p16_cb_deepen|Municipal Connections in the Negev|Decentralisation will fail if local institutions lack the means to deliver services. Better municipal connections in the Negev will give councils practical responsibilities and resources, rather than merely transferring obligations away from the centre.
p16_finish|A State of Free Citizens|HaOlam HaZeh's programme seeks a political public life in which citizens can question the state without losing their place in it. Its chosen economic and municipal institutions must show that civil freedom can also support competent government and useful development.
p17_ea_deepen|Planned Cultivation in the Negev|Agricultural expansion must account for water and land constraints before new districts are laid out. A carefully planned Negev investment will test whether productive cultivation can proceed without treating the landscape as an unlimited reserve for development.
p17_eb_deepen|Industrial Repair and Resource Efficiency|An environmental programme can improve existing industry rather than simply oppose further building. Better repair and production methods will seek more useful output from the facilities already in place and strengthen the case for technically informed conservation.
p17_ca_deepen|Conservation in Public Works|Municipal conservation plans need to influence how infrastructure is actually delivered. Applying them to public works will connect the environmental league's proposals to the decisions through which towns use land, services and building resources.
p17_cb_deepen|Healthier Services for the Development Towns|The quality of local services is part of the environment in which people live. Investment in development-town infrastructure and health administration will give the league's social programme a practical connection to household needs.
p17_finish|A Livable Development Strategy|The environmental league must show that protection of land and water can guide development rather than suspend it. Its record in agriculture, industrial methods and municipal provision will determine whether conservation becomes a dependable part of public administration.
p18_ea_deepen|Cooperative Production in the Negev|Mapam's cooperative commitments require investment in the institutions that sustain rural work. A Negev agricultural district will give the independent left a concrete project through which shared ownership can be judged by practical production and local development.
p18_eb_deepen|Applied Education for the Development Towns|Education must connect neglected communities to the skills and services needed for productive work. Applied teaching and local infrastructure will give Mapam's municipal programme a stronger foundation in the development towns.
p18_ca_deepen|Elected Scrutiny of a Peace Settlement|A negotiated peace must rest on public institutions able to scrutinise its terms. Strengthening confidence in elected government will give the independent left a domestic basis for diplomacy without assuming that negotiations themselves guarantee agreement.
p18_cb_deepen|Municipal Rights in the Constitutional Convention|A binational constitutional project must address who can question public officials and how communities are represented. Civil rights and municipal accountability will give the proposed settlement a practical institutional content beyond its founding declaration.
p18_finish|Socialism and Equal Rights|Mapam's independent programme links cooperative production to Jewish-Arab equality and an alternative diplomatic course. It must demonstrate that these commitments can support the same government, rather than leave social policy, citizenship and peace as separate aspirations.
p19_ea_deepen|Workers' Industry in Haifa|Independent communist policy must create productive work without relying on another bloc's industrial institutions. A Haifa complex will provide a practical base for the workers' programme and expose its managers to the responsibilities of sustained production.
p19_eb_deepen|Planning Skills for Public Enterprises|Democratic planning requires information and technical judgement as well as assemblies. Improving those skills will help public enterprises coordinate production while giving workers a clearer understanding of the decisions made in their name.
p19_ca_deepen|Accountable Workers' Representation|Workers' representatives need institutions through which they can question administrators rather than merely endorse them. Auditing the state will test the independent communist commitment to parliamentary rights and the accountability of its own officeholders.
p19_cb_deepen|Councils and the Elected Legislature|Councils cannot provide a durable constitutional alternative if their authority remains undefined. A negotiated division of responsibilities with the elected legislature will give the workers' institutions a place within government while limiting rival claims to unrestricted power.
p19_finish|An Independent Workers' Republic|Maki's independent course must reconcile workers' organisation with the practical demands of governing a divided society. Its economic and constitutional choices will determine whether public enterprise and political accountability can reinforce one another without submitting to an outside model.
p20_ea_deepen|Enterprise in the Development Towns|The national opposition's economic promises must reach smaller firms outside established centres. A commercial office district in the Negev will give the development towns a concrete stake in the proposed national majority.
p20_eb_deepen|The Haifa Industrial Pact|A national industrial pact needs productive capacity behind its political commitments. Investment in Haifa will test whether employers and public authorities can carry a common development programme beyond campaign declarations.
p20_ca_deepen|Oversight of a National Parliamentary Cabinet|A national majority can strengthen government without removing parliamentary restraints. More professional administration and oversight will require the cabinet to accept scrutiny even when it claims a broad popular mandate.
p20_cb_deepen|Institutions for the Popular Mandate|A presidential popular mandate needs organisations able to sustain national mobilisation beyond an election campaign. Consolidating those institutions will strengthen the executive's political reach and bind its programme more closely to continuing popular support.
p20_finish|A National Majority Movement|The Free Center and its partners seek to turn a national opposition into a governing majority. Their chosen relationship between industry, small enterprise and executive authority will determine whether that majority remains a parliamentary coalition or becomes a presidential movement.
p21_ea_deepen|Domestic Arms Production in Tel Aviv|An autonomous defence economy requires factories as well as political resolve. A funded arms facility in Tel Aviv will give the radical alliance a concrete industrial commitment, with resources that cannot simultaneously be spent on civilian development.
p21_eb_deepen|Training for National Mobilisation|A mobilisation fund must prepare people and production to meet its demands. Practical training and support for arms construction will strengthen the radical movement's capacity to organise the country around its military priorities.
p21_ca_deepen|Civil Authority over Radical Politics|Legal limits require civilian institutions with the authority to enforce them. Strengthening those bodies will test whether the radical alliance accepts constitutional restraint when it obstructs the ambitions of its own supporters.
p21_cb_deepen|The Security Apparatus of the Radical State|An authoritarian security course will concentrate attention on the institutions that organise and protect the regime. Military coordination and the security apparatus will become a stronger foundation of political authority, binding mobilisation to the survival of the governing movement.
p21_finish|The Radical State Order|The radical alliance has chosen how far mobilisation should reshape public authority and the economy. Its final settlement must give that choice institutional form, whether through accepted legal limits or an enduring security order centred on the movement itself.
p22_ea_deepen|Emergency Procurement at Home|The emergency cabinet cannot rely entirely on supply decisions made abroad. A military factory in Tel Aviv will strengthen the domestic procurement base while committing resources to the priorities set by the armed forces.
p22_eb_deepen|Civilian Standards for Emergency Industry|Civilian engineers bring methods that the emergency administration cannot provide through command alone. Applying their standards to industrial and construction planning will help the cabinet turn urgent orders into projects that can be reliably delivered.
p22_ca_deepen|Scrutinise Emergency Procurement|Emergency authority must not make procurement immune to inspection. An audit of contracts and administration will help restore civilian confidence and test whether the cabinet is preparing to return exceptional powers rather than make them permanent.
p22_cb_deepen|A Standing Military Command Structure|Permanent military rule requires a command system able to govern beyond the immediate crisis. Consolidating that structure and its mobilisation programme will strengthen the armed forces' hold on responsibilities normally exercised by civilian institutions.
p22_finish|The Future of the Emergency Cabinet|The emergency cabinet must now give effect to its constitutional choice. A return to civilian rule and a permanent military order require different institutions, but neither can avoid answering for the procurement and mobilisation programme conducted under exceptional authority.
p23_ea_deepen|Public Works for the Southern Communities|The proposed crown must establish a public purpose beyond its ceremonial foundation. Infrastructure in the Negev will give its endowments and public-works programme a concrete connection to communities whose support cannot be secured by constitutional symbolism alone.
p23_eb_deepen|Royal Industry in Haifa|Royal industrial companies will be expected to support the new settlement through productive investment. A Haifa complex will test whether enterprises associated with the crown can serve a national development purpose rather than merely provide another source of patronage.
p23_ca_deepen|Parliamentary Scrutiny of Crown Expenditure|A parliamentary crown must accept scrutiny of the money and officials attached to it. Clear oversight will make the constitutional settlement credible by applying public standards to the institutions through which royal authority is exercised.
p23_cb_deepen|The Crown's Executive Institutions|Personal royal government requires an administration that answers directly to the sovereign. Consolidating executive institutions and national service will give that choice practical force, while making the crown responsible for the results of its own governing programme.
p23_finish|The Crown and Israeli Statehood|The new crown must live within the constitutional settlement chosen at its foundation. Its economic and public-service record will determine whether it becomes an institution sustained by parliamentary confidence or the centre of a more personal form of government."""

NARRATIVE = {key: (name, text) for key, name, text in
             (line.split('|', 2) for line in ROWS.splitlines())}
assert len(NARRATIVE) == 100


def enrich(specs):
    for key, (name, text) in NARRATIVE.items():
        assert key in specs, key
        specs[key].update(label=name, body=text)
